e part of All Honors or Big Three Winds). However, they already have F2\u00d73, so discarding F2 risks letting them make a Kong or win if they need it. **Avoid**.\n- **Discard B2**: They have a pung of B2 already \u2014 absolutely dangerous. **Never discard**.\n- **Discard T7**: I have two T7s. This could be useful for a pair or possibly a pung. But without T8/T9 or T5/T6 support, hard to build. T6 is in hand, so T6+T7 could form part of a chow. But T5 is key. Discarding one T7 might be acceptable, but holding both gives flexibility.\n- **Discard W5/W8/W9**: These character tiles are all singles. No sequences possible yet. W5 could pair with itself, but only one. W8 and W9 could form part of upper-character chow, but no W7. Unseen W7: 4 \u2014 still available, but risky to depend on.\n- **Discard J1 or J3**: Dragon tiles. No signs of others collecting dragons. Safe to discard one. J1 appears in across player\u2019s history (they played J1), so likely not collecting. Similarly, J3 was played by them \u2014 seems safe.\n- **F3**: I have one, and I previously discarded F3. The across player also discarded F3. Since they have West (F3) as seat wind, they might want a pair or pung. But they haven\u2019t Peng\u2019d it when discarded, so probably don\u2019t have two. Likely safe.\n\nBest candidates for discard: **J1**, **J3**, **F3**, **W5**, **W8**, **W9**\n\nBut note: I have multiple singles. Need to reduce deadwood. J1 and J3 are both single dragons \u2014 keeping both isn't necessary unless aiming for All Honors (unlikely). Similarly, F2 and F3 are single winds \u2014 weak.\n\nSince **F2 is highly dangerous**, and **I can safely discard F3**, which is less dangerous, but still better to remove a single honor.\n\nAlternatively, **W9** is a terminal, and I have no support for it. Same with W8, W5. But if I keep W8+W9, maybe draw W7 later? Possible, but slow.\n\nHowever, **J3** is a terminal and could be part of \u201cReversible Tiles\u201d pattern (which includes J3, B1-B9 except B6/B7, T2,T4-T6,T8-T9). My hand has T3,T4,T6,T7,B2,B8,W5,W8,W9 \u2014 mixed.\n\nBut \u201cReversible Tiles\u201d requires only specific tiles. Check: allowed tiles: B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B8,B9, T2,T4,T5,T6,T8,T9, J3.\n\nMy hand includes: B2, B8, T4, T6, T7 (invalid), W5 (invalid), W8 (invalid), etc. T7 is not reversible \u2192 invalid. So cannot form Reversible Tiles.\n\nNo strong pattern emerging.\n\nBetter to focus on reducing risk and simplifying.\n\nBest discard: **J1** \u2014 it's a dragon, not needed for any immediate chow, and no sign anyone is collecting red dragons. Across player discarded J1 earlier, so likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, **W5** is a middle tile, no use, and not dangerous. Also acceptable.\n\nBut **J1** is more expendable than character terminals.\n\nWait \u2014 **Play F3**: I have F3, and the across player has West (F3) as seat wind. They discarded F3 once, didn't Peng. So unlikely they have two. Probably safe.\n\nBut safer than discarding F2? Yes, but F2 is in the valid action list \u2014 **Play F2** is listed, but **very dangerous**.\n\nSo among safe discards: J1, J3, W5, W8, W9, F3.\n\nNow consider future potential:\n\n- If I keep T3,T4,T6,T7(\u00d72), I could aim for Pure Shifted Chows or Mixed Triple Chow.\n- T3-T4 + T6-T7-T7 \u2192 if I draw T5, I can form T3-T4-T5 and T5-T6-T7 (using one T7), leaving one T7 for another set.\n- Or if I draw T8, T6-T7-T8 becomes possible.\n\nBut T9 is fully discarded (unshown T9: 0), so no T8-T9-T9 possible.\n\nUnshown T8: 4 \u2014 full set available. Previous player hasn't shown much T8.\n\nSo T7-T8-T9 possible? But T9 gone \u2014 unshown T9: 0, and previous player discarded two T9s, so yes, **no T9 left** \u2014 cannot use T9.\n\nThus, **T8 and T9 are useless to others**, so **T8 is safe**, and **T7 can be used toward lower sequences**.\n\nBut I have T7\u00d72 \u2014 valuable for now.\n\nConclusion: Keep suited tiles with potential, dump single honors.\n\nBest discard: **J1** \u2014 no one wants it, it's not helping my shape, and I need to reduce honors.\n\nOther option: **W5** \u2014 middle character, no use.\n\nBut J1 is better to go.\n\nWait \u2014 **Play J3**? Also possible. But J3 is part of \"Reversible\" and slightly more flexible. J1 is pure deadweight.\n\nAlso, **Dragon Pung** (2 points) is possible if I get more J1s, but unlikely.\n\nSo **discard J1**.\n\nBut check valid actions: [Play W5; Play B2; Play J3; Play T4; Play T6; Play T7; Play W9; Play J1; Play W8; Play T3; Play B8; Play F3; Play F2]\n\nSo **Play J1** is an option.\n\nYes.\n\nAlternatively, **Play F3**: also safe.\n\nBut **J1** is safer than F3, since F3 is seat wind.\n\nEven though they haven't claimed it, better not to test fate.\n\nSo **J1 is safest honor to discard**.\n\nFinal decision: **Play J1**\n\nReason: J1 is a single dragon tile with no immediate use in my hand, and there is no indication that any opponent is collecting red dragons (J1). Discarding it minimizes risk while simplifying my hand toward a possible all-suit hand. Other options like F2 or B2 are dangerous due to opponents' melds, and suited tiles like T4, T6, T7 have potential for chows. Among safe discards, J1 is the least useful.\n\nAnswer: Play J1"}
