 specific chow is already public.\n\nHowever, **holding onto [B6,B7,B8]** gives you flexibility:\n- Potential for Pure Shifted Chows (e.g., B6-B7-B8 + others)\n- Or even Full Flush in Dots if you transition\n- But your current hand has mixed suits and multiple discards in W/T/B.\n\nYour melds: [T7x3], [T2-T3-T4], which are solid mid-range bamboo.\nYou also previously played W1, W5, W8, W9 \u2014 indicating you're clearing Characters.\n\nBest strategy now is **to minimize risk and simplify**.\n\nAmong valid actions:\n- **Play B8**: You have two B8s. Since four identical tiles count as two pairs (Tile Hog \u00d72), but you don\u2019t have Seven Pairs yet. Still, one B8 can be spared.\n- However, **B6 is weakest single tile** among the group because:\n    - You have only one B6,\n    - It\u2019s sandwiched between B7 and B8,\n    - And discarding B6 won\u2019t complete an obvious wait for others (since B6-B7-B8 is already taken).\n- But **W5 is safest overall**: Already discarded by you before (Play W5 in history), and W5 unshown count is 2 \u2014 not rare, but not in demand. No one has shown interest in W5.\n\nWait \u2014 check **unseen W5**: there are 2 left, and you hold one \u2192 only one other out. Low probability someone is waiting on W5.\n\nBut crucially: **you have only one W5**, and it doesn't connect to anything else in hand. Not part of a pair, not adjacent to W7/W8 (gap at W6). So **W5 is isolated**.\n\nCompare:\n- W7-W8 \u2192 can start a chow (W7-W8-W9), but W9 is **already gone (0 left)** \u2192 cannot form W7-W8-W9.\n- So W7 and W8 are **dead-ended**.\n\nTherefore, **W7 and W8 are also dead tiles**.\n\nSame logic applies: W9 = 0 \u2192 cannot complete W7-W8-W9.\n\nSo **W7 and W8 cannot form chows** unless paired with W6, but you don\u2019t have W6.\n\nThus, **W5, W7, W8 are all useless suited singles with no connections**.\n\nNow choose the **least dangerous** among them.\n\nCheck what others have discarded:\n- You've already played W5 \u2192 no penalty\n- Previous plays: W1, W5, W8, W9 \u2014 clearly dumping Characters\n- Others haven't Peng'd any of your W tiles \u2192 no one is collecting Character pungs\n\nSo **safe to assume no one is building Character melds**\n\nHence, **all Character discards are safe**\n\nBut now compare scoring potential:\n\nCould we aim for **All Simples (2 points)**? Yes \u2014 avoid terminals/honors.\n\nBut we have **T2, T3, T4, T7** \u2014 all simples! Only issue is honor melds? No \u2014 our own melds are all suited.\n\nWait \u2014 our hand: W5, W7, W8, T6, B6, B7, B8 \u2014 these are all **numbered tiles**, none are honors.\n\nAnd our melds: [T7\u00d73], [T2-T3-T4] \u2014 both suited, no honors.\n\nSo if we **avoid drawing/discarding into honors**, we can keep **All Simples (2 points)** alive.\n\nBut currently, **no honor tiles in hand**, good.\n\nSo **do NOT want to pick up any honor**, and avoid forming honor melds.\n\nBack to decision:\n\nBest move is to **discard a tile that cannot contribute and is safest**\n\nTop candidates:\n- **W5**: isolated, not near anything, W6 is still available (4 left), but we don\u2019t have it. Unlikely to develop.\n- **W7/W8**: together, but need W6 or W9 \u2192 W9=0 \u2192 cannot make W7-W8-W9. W6 exists, but would need W6+W7 to make chow, and we\u2019d have to draw it. Possible, but speculative.\n- **T6**: could go with T5/T7, but T7 is tripled \u2192 no more T7. T5 is available (2 left), but opponent has used T5 in [T4,T5,T6] \u2192 so they\u2019ve completed that chow. Risky? Maybe, but T6 alone is weak.\n- **B6/B7/B8**: you have four tiles here: B6, B7, B8, B8 \u2014 almost a full sequence. Strongest potential for chow formation.\n\nSo **keep B6-B7-B8 group** \u2014 highest potential value.\n\nThus, **discard from W5/W7/W8**\n\nBetween them:\n- W5: already discarded once before (history: Play W5), so likely safe\n- W7: F3 (West Wind) was just discarded by you recently (Play F3), and seat wind is West \u2192 but F3 only has 1 unshown, and someone might be waiting on it, but not relevant\n- No one has shown interest in W7\n\nBut note: **F3 (West Wind)** is your **Seat Wind**, and you just played it \u2014 risky? But you don\u2019t have a pung of it, so no benefit anyway.\n\nFinal consideration: **could we go for Seven Pairs?**\n- Current pairs: B8\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\n- Others: all singles\n- Too far off \u2014 not viable\n\nOr **Full Flush in Bamboo?**\n- Melds: [T2,T3,T4], [T7\u00d73] \u2014 that\u2019s 6 tiles\n- Hand: T6, B6, B7, B8, B8, W5, W7, W8 \u2192 only one T tile in hand (T6)\n- So unless you draw more T, impossible\n\nSimilarly, **Dots flush**? You have three melds elsewhere.\n\nNo \u2014 must stick with mixed.\n\nBest path: **minimize loss, maintain All Simples, discard least useful tile**\n\n**W5 is the most isolated** \u2014 no connection to W7/W8 (missing W6), and you\u2019ve already discarded it once.\n\nAlso, **W5 appears twice in unshown**, so not rare, but not hot.\n\nAlternatively, **W7**: same situation.\n\nBut **W8** is slightly better than W7? No \u2014 both stuck.\n\nActually, **W7 and W8 are adjacent** \u2014 if you keep both, you retain possibility of drawing W6 to form W6-W7-W8 chow.\n\nW6: 4 unshown \u2014 plenty left.\n\nSo **W7 and W8 together have potential** if you draw W6.\n\nWhereas W5 is completely isolated \u2014 no W4 or W6.\n\nTherefore, **W5 is worse to keep than W7 or W8**\n\nSo **do not discard W7 or W8** \u2014 they have latent value.\n\nThen what about **T6**?\n\nT6: could form T5-T6-T7, but T7 is already tripled \u2014 no fourth T7, and you can\u2019t use it again. Opponent has already completed [T4,T5,T6], so likely T5 is not wanted, but possible.\n\nBut you can\u2019t form T5-T6-T7 without T5, and drawing T5 is possible (2 left), but low.\n\nMoreover, keeping T6 allows **Mixed Shifted Chows** possibility, but weak.\n\nBut compared to W5 \u2014 **T6 is better connected**? Not really.\n\nWait \u2014 you have [T2,T3,T4] meld and [T7\u00d73], and T6 in hand \u2014 that\u2019s T2-T7 covered! Missing T5 and T8/T9.\n\nBut T8/T9 are gone \u2014 so no chance of full straight.\n\nBut **Pure Straight (16 points)** requires 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in **same suit**.\n\nYou have T2-T3-T4 (covers 2-3-4), not 1-2-3. And missing T1, T5, T6 for 4-5-6, and T8-T9 for 7-8-9 \u2014 but T8/T9 gone.\n\nSo **Pure Straight impossible**\n\nSimilarly, **Mixed Straight** needs 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in different suits \u2014 you lack 1-2-3 anywhere.\n\nSo no.\n\nThus, **no major pattern emerging**\n\nBack: best is to **discard W5**, as it's:\n- Isolated\n- Not adjacent to any other tile in hand\n- Already discarded once (so precedent)\n- Less potential than W6-W7-W8 or B6-B7-B8\n\nEven though W6 is available, you don\u2019t have it \u2014 so W7/W8 have *some* hope; W5 has none.\n\n**B6** is part of [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 stronger group.\n\nSo worst tile is **W5**\n\nReason: W5 is an isolated tile with no sequential connections to other tiles in hand, and cannot contribute to any chow due to gaps. In contrast, W7-W8 offer potential for a W6-W7-W8 chow if W6 is drawn, and B6-B7-B8 form a nearly complete sequence. T6 could still pair with existing melds, though weakly. Thus, W5 is the least valuable tile to retain.\n\nAnswer: Play W5"}
